Chapter: HexwarRun
  cites Games chapter HexWar

 Runtime coverage for the hex wargame engine. Every one of the
 thirteen scenarios is played to its conclusion and its result
 printed, so the turn loop, the combat resolution, the retreat paths
 and the victory scoring all execute rather than merely compiling.

 The engine had no runtime test at all until the tail-call shuffle
 miscompile was found, and hexwar-loop was the function that
 miscompiled: its fifth parameter was assigned its fourth argument,
 the player alternation stopped, and the turn counter never reached
 the limit. Every scenario below hung. A compile-only check could not
 see any of it, which is the reason this file exists rather than a
 note saying the engine builds.

 The engine is deterministic -- each scenario is seeded explicitly --
 so the turn counts and the winners are stable and are pinned in the
 expected output.

 What this pins is that every scenario reaches its own turn limit:
 the counts below are 10, 12, 15, 16, 18, 20 and 25, each matching
 the turn-limit its config declares, and it is precisely that
 advance the miscompile destroyed. Read it as nothing more.

 What it does NOT pin, and what the reader should not take from a
 green run: every scenario ends winner=Draw with A-lost=0, B-lost=0
 and both victory-point totals zero. No unit is eliminated and no
 objective is scored in any of the thirteen. That is uniform across
 seeds and maps, which is the signature of combat resolution never
 firing rather than of thirteen genuine stalemates. It is recorded
 in the register as an open question about the engine and is
 deliberately not chased here -- but a golden of identical rows
 would otherwise read as proof the combat model works, and it is
 not that.

